logy of Japan to K. Nakajo (18790164) and to Y. Kubo, and from the Japan Society for the Promotion of Science to Y. Kubo.</p><p>Olaf S. Andersen served as editor.</p></ack><ref-list><ref id="bib1"><citation citation-type="journal">Abbott, G.W., F. Sesti, I. Splawski, M.E. Buck, M.H. Lehmann, K.W. Timothy, M.T. Keating, and S.A. Goldstein. <year>1999</year>. MiRP1 forms I<sub>Kr</sub> potassium channels with HERG and is associated with cardiac arrhythmia. <source>Cell.</source> <volume>97</volume>:<fpage>175</fpage>&#x02013;187.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10219239</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib2"><citation citation-type="journal">Abbott, G.W., M.H. Butler, S. Bendahhou, M.C. Dalakas, L.J. Ptacek, and S.A. Goldstein. <year>2001</year>. MiRP2 forms potassium channels in skeletal muscle with Kv3.4 and is associated with periodic paralysis. <source>Cell.</source> <volume>104</volume>:<fpage>217</fpage>&#x02013;231.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11207363</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib3"><citation citation-type="journal">Akabas, M.H., D.A. Stauffer, M. Xu, and A. Karlin. <year>1992</year>. Acetylcholine receptor channel structure probed in cysteine-substitution mutants. <source>Science.</source> <volume>258</volume>:<fpage>307</fpage>&#x02013;310.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">1384130</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib4"><citation citation-type="journal">Barhanin, J., F. Lesage, E. Guillemare, M. Fink, M. Lazdunski, and G. Romey. <year>1996</year>. K<sub>V</sub>LQT1 and lsK (minK) proteins associate to form the I<sub>Ks</sub> cardiac potassium current. <source>Nature.</source> <volume>384</volume>:<fpage>78</fpage>&#x02013;80.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8900282</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib5"><citation citation-type="journal">Bell, D.C., H. Yao, R.C. Saenger, J.H. Riley, and S.A. Siegelbaum. <year>2004</year>. Changes in local S4 environment provide a voltage-sensing mechanism for mammalian hyperpolarization-activated HCN channels. <source>J. Gen. Physiol.</